For $\phi, \psi$:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = \size {\map {\paren {T \phi} } t - \map {\paren {T \psi} } t}
| o = \le
| r = \size {\int_{t_0}^t L \size {\map \phi s - \map \psi s} \rd s}
| c = [[Definition:Lipschitz Continuous|Lipschitz condition]]
}}
{{eqn | o = \le
| r = L h \norm {\phi - \psi}_\infty
}}
{{end-eqn}}
Choosing $h$ with $L h < 1$ makes $T$ a [[Definition:Contraction Mapping|contraction]].

By the [[Banach Fixed-Point Theorem]], $T$ has a unique fixed point, which is the unique local solution.
This is the [[Picard-Lindelöf Theorem]].
{{qed}}
